  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 137 PM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nQuiet weather abounds this afternoon with weak ridging aloft. As\na surface high departs and moves into the central Great Lakes\ntonight, we will remain under an upper level ridge, which will\nbe strengthened by a developing low-level jet tonight under warm\nsouthwest flow. Wind speeds will increase overnight, and fog is\nnot expected to be a concern tonight.\n\nOn Friday, warm air and moisture advection will continue with a\ntrough and developing surface low moving our way from the west.\nA trailing cold front associated with a low up in Canada will\ncombine with the warm air and moisture advection to trigger\nshowers and thunderstorms. The trend for storm initiation\ncontinues to be later, favoring storm development around 6 PM or\nso. Plenty ingredients will be in place for some severe storms,\nincluding: a cold front, up to ~2 kJ/kg MUCAPE (initially\nsurface-based), favorable 30-40 kt 0-6 km shear (with some low-\nlevel streamwise vorticity ingestion possible which could result\nin a low-end tornado threat), and overall plenty of moisture\nwith PWATs inching up into the 1-1.75\" range. The timing of\nstorm initiation will be crucial, as a delay may result in a\nvery narrow window for surface-based convection in the early\nevening. Large hail and damaging winds are still looking to be\nthe main threats, especially if storms become largely elevated\nby starting later into the afternoon/evening. If they can get\ngoing early enough, a low-end tornado threat exists. The\nrain/storms should be progressive enough to minimize flooding\nrisks, but with plenty of moisture available, any brief training\nof storms could result in some very localized and short-duration\nminor flooding, especially in developed areas. Storms are\nexpected to move out with the cold front a bit after midnight.\n\nThe gates will open to more fall-like temperatures following the\ncold front into Saturday with cool northwest flow developing.\nHighs are likely only to reach the 60s in Minnesota and low 70s\nin northwest Wisconsin. We will remain in this cool air mass\nthrough the weekend with otherwise pretty quiet weather.\nSaturday and Sunday nights are looking rather chilly with high\npressure slowly passing through at the surface. At this point,\nprobabilities for temperatures cold enough for frost (~36\ndegrees) are about 10-15% for the usual cold spots. So, can't\ncompletely rule it out, but the chances are very low at this\ntime. With that said, lows in the upper 30s are looking likely\nfor the usual cold spots and low 40s for most other places.\n\nGoing into Monday, it's looking like we'll get a nice surge of\nmoisture and warm air advection with Gulf of America origins.\nThis should lead to more of a widespread light to moderate rain\nwith some thunderstorms as well (though instability isn't\ncurrently looking overly impressive for anything strong or\nsevere). There is some potential for locally heavy rainfall\njust north of the surface warm front, and there's some\nuncertainty where that might set up. Wisconsin is looking to be\nthe main target right now, but whether means northwest Wisconsin\nor central Wisconsin is still up in the air. There could be some\nlocalized minor flooding issues with this, and part of that will\ndepend on how much rain falls with Friday evening's storms (as\nof now, it looks like northeast Minnesota has the best chances,\nwhich is good news as that would spread the moisture somewhat\nevenly). Overall, this should be good news for ongoing drought\nwith a good half inch of rain minimum for most places and >1\" of\nrain very possible especially in northwest Wisconsin.\n\nAnother reinforcing cold front will pass through going into\nTuesday. Some fall-style wraparound showers are possible\nTuesday, then high pressure is likely to bring quiet but cool\nweather Wednesday and Thursday.",
